<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="xylan biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5800" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: Xylans Xylan| is a general name given for polysaccharides composed of xylose residues. The most common form of xylan is |FRAME: 1-4-beta-Xylan|, which is found in the cell walls of plants and some algae, where it is the major constitutent of |FRAME: Hemicelluloses|. |FRAME: 1-4-beta-Xylan &quot; (1&amp;rarr;4)-&amp;beta;-D-xylan&quot;| is a polymer of |FRAME: BETA-D-XYLOSE| residues connected by (1&amp;rarr;4) links. Pure xylan, consisting of only xylopyranosyl residues, is rare. Most forms of |FRAME: 1-4-beta-Xylan| are decorated with other types of sugars, in the form of |FRAME: Arabinoxylan arabinoxylan|, |FRAME: Glucuronoxylans glucuronoxylan|, |FRAME: Glucuronoarabinoxylans glucuronoarabinoxylan| or |FRAME: Arabinoglucuronoxylans arabinoglucuronoxylan|. In woody species the reducing end of the xylan molecule is capped with a unique tetrasaccharide consisting of &amp;beta;-D-Xylp-(1&amp;rarr;3)-&amp;alpha;-L-Rhap-(1&amp;rarr;2)-&amp;alpha;-D-GalpA-(1&amp;rarr;4)-D-Xylp |CITS:[Johansson77][Andersson83]|. Our current understanding of the biosynthesis of xylan is rather minimal. A number of genes from Arabidopsis, poplar and some other plants have been shown to be associated with xylan biosynthesis. In the Arabidopsis genome, four glycosyltransferases of the GT43 family, IRX9/I9H and IRX14/I14H, have been shown to be required for the normal elongation of xylan backbone |CITS: [17944810][17322407][20595206][17938130][20424005][20335400]|. It has been shown that the poplar GT43A/B/E genes are functional orthologs of the Arabidopsis IRX9, and that the GT43C/D genes are functional orthologs of IRX14 |CITS: [21596688]|. The Arabidopsis thaliana FRA8/F8H and PARVUS genes |CITS: [16272433][19224953][17991630]|, as well as the orthologous poplar GT47C and GT8E/F genes |CITS: [16887843][19789274][19395414]| and the GT8D gene |CITS: [21596688]| are involvedl in the biosynthesis of the xylan reducing end sequence.</Description>
